metal2vulkan: `OutputPrecision` on a fragment colour encoding.

Metal: on AGX the PBE15 (XR) pixel write is a Half4 partition transfer, the
f16 `pixel.write` intrinsic the AGXCompilerCore format table selects for the
Half4/USC1 configuration:
a Float32 fragment output reaches the encoder rounded to binary16, whether
or not the slot blends.
Before: the pass rounded to binary16 only inside a binary16
`ProgrammableBlend`. An unblended or replacement write quantized the Float32
value directly, so one colour could store two different codes depending on
the blend state of the draw that wrote it.
Now: `FragmentColorEncoding` carries `precision`. `Binary16` emits one
`OpQuantizeToF16` per channel after the blend, if any, and before the
channel encoders; a binary16 blend result is already on that grid. `Float32`
is the previous behaviour and what every existing caller test declares.
Test: `fragment_color::tests::a_binary16_output_precision_rounds_every_channel_before_its_encoder`
(four roundings precede the first encoder, a Float32-arithmetic blend under
the binary16 precision rounds its result once; fails to compile before, the
field does not exist). The GPU proof is reims-xr-binary16-partition.patch.

